# File Naming Convention

All files, regardless of extension, must use **kebab-case**.

## Rules

- Use lowercase letters only.
- Separate words with a single hyphen (`-`).
- No underscores (`_`), no camelCase, no PascalCase, no spaces.
- Numbers are allowed but should not start a filename.
- The extension itself is unaffected (keep `.tsx`, `.test.ts`, `.d.ts`, etc. as-is).

## Examples

✅ Correct
- `user-profile.tsx`
- `use-auth-hook.ts`
- `api-client.ts`
- `payment-summary-card.tsx`
- `date-utils.test.ts`
- `button-group.module.css`

❌ Incorrect
- `UserProfile.tsx`
- `useAuthHook.ts`
- `apiClient.ts`
- `payment_summary_card.tsx`
- `DateUtils.test.ts`
- `ButtonGroup.module.css`

## Exceptions

- Standard root-level config/meta files may keep their conventional names
  (e.g. `README.md`, `LICENSE`, `Dockerfile`, `CHANGELOG.md`).
- Framework-mandated filenames (e.g. Next.js `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`,
  `[id].tsx` dynamic routes) are exempt since the name is structurally required.

## When generating or renaming files

- Always propose kebab-case filenames when creating new files.
- If asked to rename an existing non-conforming file, suggest the kebab-case
  equivalent and update all imports/references accordingly.
- Component files still use PascalCase for the **exported component name**
  inside the file — only the filename itself must be kebab-case
  (e.g. file `user-profile.tsx` exports `export function UserProfile() {}`).